# Button bounds stay put

A button's width and height come from its resting face: the label, the icon, and the padding.
Icon-only controls use their fixed box. A loading spinner, or any other content that appears in
that button, is drawn inside the resting box. The button does not grow or shrink to fit it.

## Why

React Native's small `ActivityIndicator` lays out at 20×20. The `sm` label line is 16, and `md`
is 18. An in-flow spinner beside the label is taller than the text and adds its own width, so a
control such as Subscribe / Unsubscribe stretches while the request runs.

Web `Button` already holds its size: the label stays in the layout (invisible) and the spinner is
absolutely centered inside a fixed-height control.

## Do

- Keep the resting label and icon mounted while a spinner or other stand-in is showing. Hide them
  visually. Their layout is what holds the width and height.
- Center the stand-in on that face (`position: 'absolute'`). It is not a flex sibling of the label.
- Fit the spinner in the resting content slot — the label's line height, or the icon-only box.
  Scale it down when 20×20 is taller than that slot, and clip paint to the button interior so a
  native indicator cannot draw outside the pill.
- Set `accessibilityState.busy` (mobile) or `aria-busy` (web) while loading. The accessible name
  stays the resting label.
- When one control swaps between resting labels of different lengths (Subscribe / Unsubscribe),
  size it to the wider label so that swap does not jump either. Keep the inactive string in the
  layout and hide it without adding height.
- Use shared `Button` `loading` for this. A one-off pressable follows the same overlay contract.

## Don't

- Don't render `ActivityIndicator` (or any status glyph) as a flex sibling of the label.
- Don't unmount the label or icon for the loading state.
- Don't size the spinner larger than the content slot and let the button expand around it.
- Don't build a control that grows when an icon, count, or status glyph appears inside it.

